# PreToolUse hook: hold `nix run .#gate` to its approved invocations.
#
# The gate names one way to start a run and one way to read it. That
# text is context rather than enforcement, so a session can compose a
# variant anyway, and a pipe into `tail` holds every line until the run
# stops. This hook is the enforcement that text is not, over the
# spellings a session writes by hand.
#
# It lives beside the gate rather than beside either tree's doctrine,
# because the gate is the thing it constrains and both halves of a
# nested checkout run the same one.
set -euo pipefail

invocation=$(jq -r '.tool_input.command // ""')

# Each line is tested on its own, because a command is a list of lines
# and the gate can start on any of them. Testing the whole string at
# once let a newline carry the run past both tests below: the pre-filter
# looked for a separator it never found, and the approved form is
# anchored to the ends of what it is given.
#
# The flake reference that names this app. It is `.#gate` from this
# repository's own root, and a path to this repository from the root
# above it, which in a pair is `./ubos-main#gate`. Both name the same
# app, and the script resolves the whole checkout from the caller's
# directory rather than from the flake's, so both gate both trees.
#
# It is one name because the two patterns below have to agree about
# what a gate invocation looks like. A ref the pre-filter knows and the
# approved form does not would refuse every spelling of it.
gate_ref='\.(/[A-Za-z0-9._-]+)*#gate'

# The pre-filter has to fire on every spelling that starts a gate, and
# the approved form below is what narrows those down again. So it errs
# wide: a spelling it fires on and the approved form does not admit is
# refused, which is the safe direction.
#
# Command position is the test: the start of a line, or just after a
# separator. The separators are the ones that compose a call — `&&`,
# `||`, `;`, a pipe, an opening subshell and an opening brace group. A
# brace group was the hole this list had: a run wrapped in one, or in a
# function body, reached the shell with its pipe intact.
#
# A no-op wrapper is a word before `nix` that changes nothing about the
# command: `command`, `time`, `env` and `builtin`. A pattern anchored
# to the literal `nix` never saw the `nix` standing behind one, so
# `time nix run .#gate | tail` composed a run this hook missed. The
# approved form admits no wrapper, so a bare `time nix run .#gate` is
# refused too. That is over-refusal of something harmless, and it keeps
# one spelling.
#
# What no test of a line can catch is a run composed inside a second
# shell. `bash -c`, `sh -c`, `eval` and `xargs` each build their
# command from a string, and that string is data here. Those spellings
# reach the shell with a pipe intact. This hook holds the spellings a
# session writes directly, which is what a session writes; it is not a
# sandbox, and nothing here should be read as one.
#
# A heredoc that writes these commands into a file is refused as well,
# because its body lines begin at the start of a line and nothing here
# tells a body apart from a command. That is over-refusal rather than a
# bypass, so it is the safe direction, and writing such a file needs a
# tool other than the shell.
#
# The name ends where the pre-filter says it ends. Without the trailing
# boundary the ref matched as a prefix, so `nix run .#gateway` fired the
# pre-filter, failed the approved form, and was refused under the gate's
# own message. The boundary belongs here and not in the ref, because the
# approved form splices the ref against its own subcommands and would
# then need a second separator where it already has one. A separator is
# a boundary as much as a space is: `{ nix run .#gate; }` ends the name
# with the semicolon, and with only whitespace admitted there the brace
# group this list was widened for still reached the shell.
in_command_position="(^|[&|;({])[[:space:]]*((command|time|env|builtin)[[:space:]]+)*([A-Z_]+=[^[:space:]]+[[:space:]]+)*nix[[:space:]]+run[[:space:]]+$gate_ref([[:space:]&|;)}]|\$)"

# The three subcommands of nix/gate.nix, with what may stand around
# them. Nothing here composes: each addition is a fixed word or a
# number, and none of them holds the run's output back.
#
# No `cd` prefix is admitted, because none is needed: the ref above
# names this app from the root above as well as from this one. A
# `cd ubos-main && nix run .#gate` is therefore refused, and the
# refusal names the spelling that replaces it. That form worked, so
# this is a real refusal rather than an over-refusal, and it is what
# leaves one spelling for each root instead of two for one of them.
#
# A trailing `2>&1` is a redirection and not a pipe, so the exit status
# stays the gate's own. It is how an agent reads a refusal, which the
# gate writes to stderr, beside the table it writes to stdout.
#
# The two overrides may repeat, because each is one name and one
# integer: a repeat changes which value the shell exports and never the
# command that runs.
#
# A check name carries a slash whenever it came from a submodule, which
# is every check of the inner tree, so the log form has to admit one.
# Without it the hook refused the only spelling that reaches half the
# gate's failures.
approved="^[[:space:]]*(GATE_MAX_JOBS=[0-9]+ |GATE_CORES=[0-9]+ )*nix run $gate_ref( -- status| -- log [A-Za-z0-9._/-]+)?( 2>&1)?[[:space:]]*\$"

refuse() {
  cat >&2 <<'MESSAGE'
ERROR: This is not an approved invocation of the gate.

The gate app belongs to this repository. Name it with the reference that
is correct from the directory you are in. Both of them gate every tree of
the checkout, so there is nothing to change directory for:

  nix run .#gate              from this repository's root
  nix run ./ubos-main#gate    from the root above it, in a pair

Read a run that is going, from this turn or from a later one:

  nix run .#gate -- status

Read one failing check:

  nix run .#gate -- log <check>

You can put `GATE_MAX_JOBS=<number>` or `GATE_CORES=<number>` before
`nix run`, and `2>&1` after the command. Add nothing else.

Do not add a pipe, a redirect to a file, or a trailing ampersand. A pipe
keeps all of the output until the run stops. It also makes the exit
status the last command's and not the gate's. To start the gate and
continue to work, run it in the background. Then read the table later
with `-- status`.
MESSAGE
  exit 2
}

# The quotes around the flake reference come off before either test. A
# quoted attribute is the same invocation to the shell, so it has to
# reach the approved form rather than slip the pre-filter, which is what
# `nix run '.#gate' | tail` did while the pattern held the bare spelling
# only.
#
# Every other quoted region goes whole, with its quotes. Stripping the
# quotes alone turned data into a command: a separator inside a quoted
# argument, as in `echo 'cd ubos-main && nix run .#gate'`, was left
# standing before `nix` once its quotes were gone, and prose about the
# gate was refused as a run of it. Nothing inside quotes composes, so
# dropping the region loses no pipe the shell would see, and a quote
# with no partner is left as it is, which is the safe direction.
quoted_ref="['\"]($gate_ref)['\"]"
quoted="'[^']*'|\"[^\"]*\""
while IFS= read -r line; do
  while [[ $line =~ $quoted_ref ]]; do
    line=${line/"${BASH_REMATCH[0]}"/${BASH_REMATCH[1]}}
  done
  while [[ $line =~ $quoted ]]; do
    line=${line/"${BASH_REMATCH[0]}"/}
  done
  if [[ $line =~ $in_command_position ]] && [[ ! $line =~ $approved ]]; then
    refuse
  fi
done <<< "$invocation"

exit 0
